Title: Vultures
Order: 4
Production Code: 1x04

EXT. SABLE VERGE - OPEN SPACE NEAR HALYARD - DAY

AMB: open comms static, a ship's engine holding position too close

Boxed text:

"The ship that drops out of the lane behind you isn't running Concern colors, Choir markings, or a Compact transponder. It doesn't need to announce what it is. Everyone in the Verge already knows Hollow Chain paint when they see it."

DRAY USHANT (V.O.)
(over comms, easy, unbothered)
No need for anyone to get clever. Word's gone around you're sitting on something interesting. I'm here to make you a fair offer before someone less polite does.

CASS IBARRA
And if we say no?

DRAY USHANT (V.O.)
Then I make a less fair one. Your choice, Captain. Genuinely.

GM note: play this as a real negotiation, not a foregone fight — Ushant only escalates to force if talking fails, and would much rather buy the Key than risk the ship taking damage over it.

If the party stalls, refuses, or tries to run:

SFX: a warning shot, close enough to read the paint on the hull

DRAY USHANT (V.O.) (CONT'D)
That one missed on purpose. Next one's a question of how good my gunner's having a day.

[[Nine]] can attempt to get [[the Loose Thread]] clear, or the party can hand over something worthless dressed up as the Key, or they can simply fight. Whatever they choose, the Chain backs off once it's clear the cost of pressing further outweighs the prize — Ushant is a businessman first.

DRAY USHANT (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(pulling back, almost admiring)
Fine. Keep it. But you'll want to figure out what you're holding before somebody with less patience than me comes asking.

MUSIC: OUT

Boxed text, after:

"Whatever happened out here, one thing's clear now — this isn't a secret anymore. Every faction in the Verge either knows you have the Key, or will by the time you're back at dock."

GM note — contingencies: if the party attacks Ushant's ship preemptively rather than letting him make his offer, he retreats rather than dies here — he's a recurring faction leader, not a boss fight, and should only actually die if the party specifically hunts him down later with that intent. If the party simply hands the Key over to end the scene quickly, let them — the campaign survives fine without it; redirect the Choir's and Concern's attention toward whoever the Chain sells it to instead.
